00:01:53.330 --> 00:01:56.428
Our first question is about
abstaining from thoughts.

00:01:56.428 --> 00:01:57.970
And in answering
this question, we're

00:01:57.970 --> 00:01:59.680
going to skirt right
up against a topic

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:01.660
that I've covered before
on the podcast which

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:03.820
is obsessive compulsive
disorder, which

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:05.123
is a very serious disorder.

00:02:05.123 --> 00:02:06.790
I mean, it can have
a range of severity,

00:02:06.790 --> 00:02:09.940
but people with true
OCD suffer a lot

00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:13.150
from obsessions, these
are intrusive thoughts,

00:02:13.150 --> 00:02:17.860
and compulsions, which are the
behaviors typically associated

00:02:17.860 --> 00:02:18.910
with those thoughts.

00:02:18.910 --> 00:02:21.040
I think it's really
important that we define

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:24.220
OCD really clearly so
that we can make sure

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:26.980
that we're talking
about intrusive thoughts

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:29.950
and the desire to abstain
from thoughts versus OCD

00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:31.840
and where there might
be some overlap there.

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:36.280
The functional definition
of OCD that really pertains

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:39.730
to the disorder, obsessive
compulsive disorder,

00:02:39.730 --> 00:02:42.880
OCD as opposed to we
call people neurotic,

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:44.710
or we say you have
OCD, or I'm so

00:02:44.710 --> 00:02:46.540
OCD about this,
or OCD about that,

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:50.530
that's become a common
use of the acronym OCD.

00:02:50.530 --> 00:02:55.180
But true OCD is a
situation in which

00:02:55.180 --> 00:02:58.600
engaging in a particular
compulsive behavior

00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:04.360
does not serve to reduce the
intensity or the frequency

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:05.260
of the obsessions.

00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:07.070
In fact, it makes it worse.

00:03:07.070 --> 00:03:09.880
So the typical thing
that we hear these days

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:13.390
is oh, you're so OCD, or
he's so OCD, or she's so OCD,

00:03:13.390 --> 00:03:15.250
or I'm so OCD.

00:03:15.250 --> 00:03:19.000
Now if someone is OCD in
that sense, OK, so not

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:20.902
the clinical disorder
but OCD in the sense

00:03:20.902 --> 00:03:23.110
that they're really clean,
they're really fastidious,

00:03:23.110 --> 00:03:24.670
they need everything perfect.

00:03:24.670 --> 00:03:29.080
However, that person can achieve
some level of calm and comfort

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:31.060
that's fairly long
lasting if everything's

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:32.860
clean and perfect,
well, then that's not

00:03:32.860 --> 00:03:34.210
obsessive compulsive disorder.

00:03:34.210 --> 00:03:35.982
This is really
important to understand

00:03:35.982 --> 00:03:37.690
as we venture into
answering the question

00:03:37.690 --> 00:03:39.940
about intrusive
thoughts and the desire

00:03:39.940 --> 00:03:41.920
to abstain from
certain thoughts.

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:45.910
A person with true OCD will
keep cleaning and cleaning,

00:03:45.910 --> 00:03:48.640
or washing their hands,
or arranging things

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:50.860
at perfect right angles,
but the more they

00:03:50.860 --> 00:03:53.650
do it, the more their
anxiety goes up.

00:03:53.650 --> 00:03:57.670
A person who has more typical,
gosh, we don't really even

00:03:57.670 --> 00:03:59.830
have a language for this
nowadays unfortunately,

00:03:59.830 --> 00:04:03.940
but the sort of person that's
extremely particular, they need

00:04:03.940 --> 00:04:06.970
things a certain way, and
they're very driven to resolve

00:04:06.970 --> 00:04:09.520
things and do things to make
sure that things are done

00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:13.040
in a certain way, or
handled in a certain way,

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:15.340
well, if that person
can experience relief

00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:19.029
from engaging in let's say,
exercise, or from cleaning,

00:04:19.029 --> 00:04:20.860
you'd say you're so
OCD about exercise.

00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:23.940
Well, but if exercising for
an hour or even two hours

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:26.440
in the morning make somebody
really calm the rest of the day

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:27.982
and they don't need
to exercise well,

00:04:27.982 --> 00:04:31.270
then they're not
necessarily true OCD.

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:34.880
They don't necessarily have a
disorder related to exercise.

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:37.570
However, if they're exercising
for two hours in the morning

00:04:37.570 --> 00:04:39.340
and then they find
they can't concentrate

00:04:39.340 --> 00:04:42.100
on other things and their desire
to exercise just increases

00:04:42.100 --> 00:04:45.340
and increases and becomes
intrusive for them disrupting

00:04:45.340 --> 00:04:48.160
their quality of life
throughout the day,

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:51.160
well, then that's falling
under the umbrella of what

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:52.880
we would call a true disorder.

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:55.720
So we want to highlight
that because the question is

00:04:55.720 --> 00:04:58.210
about a desire to
abstain from thoughts,

00:04:58.210 --> 00:05:00.893
and the question is,
in your discussion

00:05:00.893 --> 00:05:02.560
with Dr. Anna Lembke--
and I should just

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:05.500
mention Dr. Anna Lembke is the
director of our Dual Diagnosis

00:05:05.500 --> 00:05:08.310
Addiction Clinic at Stanford
University School of Medicine.

00:05:08.310 --> 00:05:10.800
She's the author of this
incredible book Dopamine Nation

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:12.600
that I think
everybody should read.

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:14.490
I do believe everyone
should read that book.

00:05:14.490 --> 00:05:17.400
So important for the
addict and nonaddict

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:20.680
alike because it deals with
basically the state of our life

00:05:20.680 --> 00:05:21.180
nowadays.

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:23.040
We're living in this
dopamine rich world

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:25.680
where we can quickly become
dopamine depleted, which

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:28.470
is terrible, leads to
depression in addition to things

00:05:28.470 --> 00:05:30.270
like addiction in
some folks, et cetera.

00:05:30.270 --> 00:05:31.780
Anna was a guest on the podcast.

00:05:31.780 --> 00:05:34.260
You can find her episode
at hubermanlab.com.

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:35.093
Just put Anna.

00:05:35.093 --> 00:05:36.510
I think she's the
only guest we've

00:05:36.510 --> 00:05:40.050
had thus far whose name is Anna,
so it'll just pop up there.

00:05:40.050 --> 00:05:41.520
The entire website
is searchable.

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:43.830
But if you want to
listen to that episode

00:05:43.830 --> 00:05:46.650
or watch that episode, you
can access it hubermanlab.com.

00:05:46.650 --> 00:05:49.230
The question is
in your discussion

00:05:49.230 --> 00:05:51.990
with Dr. Anna Lembke you
were talking about abstaining

00:05:51.990 --> 00:05:54.150
from people's drug
of choice for 30 days

00:05:54.150 --> 00:05:56.100
to reset the dopamine
reward pathway.

00:05:56.100 --> 00:05:58.950
And indeed that is the
prescriptive that Dr. Anna

00:05:58.950 --> 00:06:01.020
Lembke gives for
most all addictions

00:06:01.020 --> 00:06:04.050
and for people that are
suffering from milder even

00:06:04.050 --> 00:06:06.240
severe depression
related to dopamine

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:10.950
seeking behaviors, social
media, video games, sex, food,

00:06:10.950 --> 00:06:11.460
et cetera.

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:14.250
However, of course, in
the instance of food

00:06:14.250 --> 00:06:18.270
or in some other addictions,
you can't abstain for 30 days.

00:06:18.270 --> 00:06:21.210
That would be terrible to
abstain for food for 30 days.

00:06:21.210 --> 00:06:24.240
I'm sure people have done it,
but it is not healthy to do.

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:26.280
Certain things like
alcohol and drugs people

00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:28.050
can abstain for
30 days in efforts

00:06:28.050 --> 00:06:30.060
to never go back to them again.

00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:33.060
There are cases of
severe alcohol or opiate

00:06:33.060 --> 00:06:37.090
dependence where people can't go
cold turkey or they risk dying,

00:06:37.090 --> 00:06:39.960
so there they need to really
work with a physician.

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:44.100
But the 30-day reset
of the dopamine system

00:06:44.100 --> 00:06:45.690
is something that's
very real and that

00:06:45.690 --> 00:06:47.080
pertains to most people.

00:06:47.080 --> 00:06:49.080
That is it's going to be
very effective for most

00:06:49.080 --> 00:06:51.130
people struggling with
either behavioral,

00:06:51.130 --> 00:06:53.130
or what are sometimes
called process addictions,

00:06:53.130 --> 00:06:55.950
or other types of addictions
including substance abuse

00:06:55.950 --> 00:06:57.150
issues.

00:06:57.150 --> 00:06:58.200
The question continues.

00:06:58.200 --> 00:06:59.700
You said you were
going to ask Ana

00:06:59.700 --> 00:07:01.890
how to abstain if
your drug of choice

00:07:01.890 --> 00:07:04.530
is a thought or
narrative as opposed

00:07:04.530 --> 00:07:07.530
to a substance or a behavior.

00:07:07.530 --> 00:07:09.690
The question continues,
as far as I can tell,

00:07:09.690 --> 00:07:11.190
your discussion
with Ana moved on

00:07:11.190 --> 00:07:13.230
before you asked
her the question.

00:07:13.230 --> 00:07:15.090
Would you be able to
discuss strategies

00:07:15.090 --> 00:07:17.740
for abstaining from addictive
thoughts and narratives?

00:07:17.740 --> 00:07:18.240
OK.

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:19.865
So let's talk about
intrusive thoughts,

00:07:19.865 --> 00:07:22.770
and let's start off by asking
ourselves, what is a thought?

00:07:22.770 --> 00:07:24.780
In the realm of
neurobiology, we have

00:07:24.780 --> 00:07:29.320
sensations which are the
processes by which our neurons,

00:07:29.320 --> 00:07:34.500
nerve cells convert light,
photons, sound waves, touch,

00:07:34.500 --> 00:07:38.620
mechanical pressure, et
cetera into electrical signals

00:07:38.620 --> 00:07:42.410
and chemical signals that
head into our nervous system,

00:07:42.410 --> 00:07:43.270
including our brain.

00:07:43.270 --> 00:07:46.360
And then our brain, at least
we think, make sense of them,

00:07:46.360 --> 00:07:49.520
and help us navigate
the world that we're in.

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:52.990
So the sensation part
is a pure transformation

00:07:52.990 --> 00:07:56.110
of mechanical information,
or in some cases,

00:07:56.110 --> 00:07:58.420
chemical information like smell.

00:07:58.420 --> 00:08:00.970
Literally, volatile chemicals
floating around in the world

00:08:00.970 --> 00:08:03.460
are brought in through
your nose and your neurons

00:08:03.460 --> 00:08:05.260
and your olfactory
bulb convert those

00:08:05.260 --> 00:08:06.760
into electrical and
chemical signals

00:08:06.760 --> 00:08:09.610
that your brain can understand.

00:08:09.610 --> 00:08:15.580
Perceptions are your idea
of what those signals out

00:08:15.580 --> 00:08:16.907
in the world are.

00:08:16.907 --> 00:08:18.490
Either because you're
paying attention

00:08:18.490 --> 00:08:21.100
to them, or for some
other reason you decide

00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:24.160
that stop sign in
front of you is red,

00:08:24.160 --> 00:08:27.970
and that the sign
behind it is white,

00:08:27.970 --> 00:08:32.110
and that the sky is blue or
cloudy, those are perceptions.

00:08:32.110 --> 00:08:34.960
So we have sensations
and perceptions.

00:08:34.960 --> 00:08:38.500
Then we have emotions
which are these things that

00:08:38.500 --> 00:08:41.830
include the mind and
body that are related

00:08:41.830 --> 00:08:44.200
to neuromodulators like
dopamine, serotonin, et cetera,

00:08:44.200 --> 00:08:46.460
although, those chemicals
do other things as well.

00:08:46.460 --> 00:08:48.220
And then, of course, we
have behaviors, actions.

00:08:48.220 --> 00:08:50.410
Everything from me moving
my pen on a piece of paper

00:08:50.410 --> 00:08:54.150
to walking, running, et cetera.

00:08:54.150 --> 00:08:58.590
Thoughts are a fifth
category of neural

00:08:58.590 --> 00:09:01.710
functioning that we
really need to define,

00:09:01.710 --> 00:09:04.620
but that doesn't have
as strict a definition

00:09:04.620 --> 00:09:08.880
as sensations, perceptions,
or feeling, or action.

00:09:08.880 --> 00:09:10.212
But thoughts are real.

00:09:10.212 --> 00:09:10.920
We all know this.

00:09:10.920 --> 00:09:13.950
Thoughts are real, and one way
that we can define thoughts

00:09:13.950 --> 00:09:19.860
is that thoughts are
perceptions that include data

00:09:19.860 --> 00:09:25.500
from the past, present,
or future, or combinations

00:09:25.500 --> 00:09:30.438
of past and present, or present
and future, or future and past.

00:09:30.438 --> 00:09:32.980
I'm not trying to give an overly
complicated definition here,

00:09:32.980 --> 00:09:35.880
but if we are going to
answer a question about how

00:09:35.880 --> 00:09:38.040
to abstain from
thoughts in a really

00:09:38.040 --> 00:09:39.990
direct and actionable
way, we really

00:09:39.990 --> 00:09:41.700
need to address
what our thoughts.

00:09:41.700 --> 00:09:45.270
So thoughts are perceptions
that are generated internally.

00:09:45.270 --> 00:09:47.535
We don't need any
external sensation

00:09:47.535 --> 00:09:48.660
in order to have a thought.

00:09:48.660 --> 00:09:49.577
We can close our eyes.

00:09:49.577 --> 00:09:52.200
We could be in sensory
isolation for that matter.

00:09:52.200 --> 00:09:54.120
We could even be
floating with no gravity,

00:09:54.120 --> 00:09:56.670
and we can have a thought.

00:09:56.670 --> 00:10:01.100
Thoughts tend to run pretty much
automatically in the background

00:10:01.100 --> 00:10:01.800
all the time.

00:10:01.800 --> 00:10:03.440
Some people refer
to this as chatter

00:10:03.440 --> 00:10:06.560
in the back of our minds
in a very unstructured way.

00:10:06.560 --> 00:10:08.480
And then if we
force our thoughts

00:10:08.480 --> 00:10:11.420
to be structured because
we decide they need to be,

00:10:11.420 --> 00:10:13.640
or if something
in our environment

00:10:13.640 --> 00:10:17.583
captures our perception,
then our thoughts

00:10:17.583 --> 00:10:18.500
tend to be structured.

00:10:18.500 --> 00:10:19.770
Let me give you an example.

00:10:19.770 --> 00:10:22.250
If I'm just walking
around, if there

00:10:22.250 --> 00:10:24.410
were a way in which
we could broadcast

00:10:24.410 --> 00:10:27.410
my thoughts onto a screen,
I hope we wouldn't do that.

00:10:27.410 --> 00:10:30.290
But those thoughts
would be a mixture

00:10:30.290 --> 00:10:33.350
of semi-complete
sentences and gibberish.

00:10:33.350 --> 00:10:37.033
However if somebody
stops me, and says, hi.

00:10:37.033 --> 00:10:37.700
Good to see you.

00:10:37.700 --> 00:10:38.640
What are you doing today?

00:10:38.640 --> 00:10:40.515
And I start answering,
well, then my thoughts

00:10:40.515 --> 00:10:43.190
are suddenly being driven
by an external stimulus,

00:10:43.190 --> 00:10:46.700
their question, and some
internal ideas, memory

00:10:46.700 --> 00:10:48.920
of who I am and what
I'm doing that day so

00:10:48.920 --> 00:10:50.240
past and present, and so on.

00:10:50.240 --> 00:10:52.070
And so thoughts
become structured.

00:10:52.070 --> 00:10:55.890
So when we have a question about
how to abstain from thoughts,

00:10:55.890 --> 00:10:57.680
we need to be
additionally specific

00:10:57.680 --> 00:11:01.010
and really pinpoint that
what we're trying to do

00:11:01.010 --> 00:11:05.720
is to abstain from thoughts we
don't want presumably, either

00:11:05.720 --> 00:11:08.660
because they are too
repetitive and distracting,

00:11:08.660 --> 00:11:12.800
or because what's contained in
those thoughts is disturbing.

00:11:12.800 --> 00:11:15.950
This is important because
it gives us two answers

00:11:15.950 --> 00:11:19.580
to the questions that
are highly divergent.

00:11:19.580 --> 00:11:21.170
One answer to the
question of how

00:11:21.170 --> 00:11:23.420
to abstain from intrusive
thoughts, thoughts that

00:11:23.420 --> 00:11:27.350
were addicted to, is if
those thoughts are merely

00:11:27.350 --> 00:11:31.430
on loop all the time
and we can't stop them,

00:11:31.430 --> 00:11:33.980
but the thoughts themselves
aren't particularly disturbing.

00:11:33.980 --> 00:11:36.710
So think about a song you
can't get out of your head,

00:11:36.710 --> 00:11:38.990
or you keep
recounting some event,

00:11:38.990 --> 00:11:41.310
but the event itself
isn't very disturbing.

00:11:41.310 --> 00:11:43.682
It's just intrusive
because it's there.

00:11:43.682 --> 00:11:45.140
Well, in that case,
the data really

00:11:45.140 --> 00:11:47.660
point to trying to
anchor your thoughts

00:11:47.660 --> 00:11:49.140
to some external stimulus.

00:11:49.140 --> 00:11:52.190
So getting into action,
getting into activities

00:11:52.190 --> 00:11:55.100
that really draw your attention
away from that thought.

00:11:55.100 --> 00:11:57.420
Now you may still hear it
scrolling in the background,

00:11:57.420 --> 00:11:59.780
so you might be sitting in
class still hearing that loop

00:11:59.780 --> 00:12:01.160
of thoughts in the background.

00:12:01.160 --> 00:12:03.560
That's something that
over time ought to wane,

00:12:03.560 --> 00:12:06.900
it ought to disappear
if we try and bring

00:12:06.900 --> 00:12:09.660
more and more attention
to whatever it

00:12:09.660 --> 00:12:12.180
is that's in our
environment, whatever

00:12:12.180 --> 00:12:16.500
it is that we happen to be
learning or doing physically,

00:12:16.500 --> 00:12:17.730
et cetera.

00:12:17.730 --> 00:12:20.160
Things like mindful meditation.

00:12:20.160 --> 00:12:22.830
Doing a 10 or even
just 5 minute a day

00:12:22.830 --> 00:12:24.797
practice of sitting
with eyes closed,

00:12:24.797 --> 00:12:26.880
or lying down with eyes
closed and really focusing

00:12:26.880 --> 00:12:30.450
on one's breath, focusing
one's attention on sometimes

00:12:30.450 --> 00:12:32.910
called the third eye
center, but in science we'd

00:12:32.910 --> 00:12:34.980
say just the region right
behind the forehead,

00:12:34.980 --> 00:12:36.563
you're directing
your attention there,

00:12:36.563 --> 00:12:40.267
has been shown to increase
focus for singular topics

00:12:40.267 --> 00:12:42.850
and can improve memory and do a
bunch of other things as well.

00:12:42.850 --> 00:12:45.610
Those are data from Dr. Wendy
Suzuki's lab at New York

00:12:45.610 --> 00:12:46.110
University.

00:12:46.110 --> 00:12:47.735
She was a guest on
the podcast as well.

00:12:47.735 --> 00:12:50.903
My laboratory has run studies
on mindful meditation as well.

00:12:50.903 --> 00:12:52.320
So what you're
really trying to do

00:12:52.320 --> 00:12:54.490
is learn how to focus
better on one thing,

00:12:54.490 --> 00:12:56.310
and by focusing
on that one thing,

00:12:56.310 --> 00:12:58.710
you focus off these
repetitive thoughts.

00:12:58.710 --> 00:13:01.320
Now, I have a feeling that
this question was asked

00:13:01.320 --> 00:13:03.180
and that many people
upvoted this question

00:13:03.180 --> 00:13:06.150
because the issue isn't
just thoughts that

00:13:06.150 --> 00:13:08.790
are intrusive because
they're there and on repeat,

00:13:08.790 --> 00:13:12.540
but because the thoughts
themselves are actually

00:13:12.540 --> 00:13:13.680
troubling.

00:13:13.680 --> 00:13:17.280
This could be recounting a
trauma, someone harmed you,

00:13:17.280 --> 00:13:22.050
you observed something that was
disturbing, you felt wronged,

00:13:22.050 --> 00:13:24.510
you felt someone
else was wronged,

00:13:24.510 --> 00:13:27.780
you can't seem to get your
mind off of something,

00:13:27.780 --> 00:13:29.560
and your emotions
tend to follow,

00:13:29.560 --> 00:13:30.660
and so it's uncomfortable.

00:13:30.660 --> 00:13:34.080
I have a feeling this is
the root of the question.

00:13:34.080 --> 00:13:36.870
In that case, the approach
is very different.

00:13:36.870 --> 00:13:39.090
What we know from essentially
all of the quality

00:13:39.090 --> 00:13:41.970
scientific and
clinical studies is

00:13:41.970 --> 00:13:44.100
that those sorts of
intrusive thoughts

00:13:44.100 --> 00:13:46.440
are very much like a trauma.

00:13:46.440 --> 00:13:48.600
Now we have to be clear in
defining what trauma is.

00:13:48.600 --> 00:13:50.142
I'll use the definition
that Dr. Paul

00:13:50.142 --> 00:13:54.420
Conti, another incredible
guest that was on our podcast.

00:13:54.420 --> 00:13:57.060
A Stanford-Harvard
trained psychiatrist.

00:13:57.060 --> 00:13:59.130
I think one of the
world's foremost leaders

00:13:59.130 --> 00:14:01.950
on the issue of trauma and
psychiatry and psychology

00:14:01.950 --> 00:14:03.570
generally.

00:14:03.570 --> 00:14:08.070
He defines trauma as an event
or something that fundamentally

00:14:08.070 --> 00:14:10.680
changes the way that your
nervous system works such

00:14:10.680 --> 00:14:14.280
that you function less
adaptively going forward

00:14:14.280 --> 00:14:15.670
from that event.

00:14:15.670 --> 00:14:19.470
So not every bad occurrence
in your life is a trauma.

00:14:19.470 --> 00:14:21.040
That's good news.

00:14:21.040 --> 00:14:23.160
The bad news is many
people have traumas,

00:14:23.160 --> 00:14:25.500
and traumas change the way
that our nervous system

00:14:25.500 --> 00:14:28.540
works so that we don't
function as well as we could.

00:14:28.540 --> 00:14:33.150
So in that sense, intrusive
thoughts that are disturbing

00:14:33.150 --> 00:14:36.810
are in many ways traumas and
are reinforcing that trauma.

00:14:36.810 --> 00:14:41.770
Now, we know that almost
counterintuitively

00:14:41.770 --> 00:14:43.270
in order to deal
with trauma, you

00:14:43.270 --> 00:14:45.310
have to get very
close to that trauma.

00:14:45.310 --> 00:14:46.720
You don't have to
reexpose, and I

00:14:46.720 --> 00:14:48.490
would hope you would
not reexpose yourself

00:14:48.490 --> 00:14:49.610
to the very same trauma.

00:14:49.610 --> 00:14:53.170
But we know that one of the
best ways to deal with traumas

00:14:53.170 --> 00:14:56.650
is to get very clear about the
narrative around those traumas.

00:14:56.650 --> 00:15:00.490
Now this can be done
with a therapist ideally,

00:15:00.490 --> 00:15:04.870
but not everyone has access to
therapy or can afford therapy.

00:15:04.870 --> 00:15:08.210
There's a range of quality of
therapists for that matter,

00:15:08.210 --> 00:15:10.840
so we're always referring
to the desire for people

00:15:10.840 --> 00:15:13.870
to do great therapy
with really great,

00:15:13.870 --> 00:15:16.490
meaning excellently
trained people.

00:15:16.490 --> 00:15:20.470
But it turns out that if you
want to extinguish an intrusive

00:15:20.470 --> 00:15:22.900
thought, one of the
best ways to do that

00:15:22.900 --> 00:15:26.620
is to journal about that
particular thought extensively.

00:15:26.620 --> 00:15:29.950
So rather than the earlier
strategy for intrusive thoughts

00:15:29.950 --> 00:15:32.290
where they're just on
loop, and intrusive

00:15:32.290 --> 00:15:34.180
because they're on
loop and present,

00:15:34.180 --> 00:15:35.950
but their content
isn't disturbing,

00:15:35.950 --> 00:15:39.220
when that thought is
disturbing and intrusive,

00:15:39.220 --> 00:15:43.240
we know that it's very
useful to script out

00:15:43.240 --> 00:15:46.750
as much detail about
that particular thought

00:15:46.750 --> 00:15:48.505
and the things around
it as possible.

00:15:48.505 --> 00:15:51.660
Now obviously, you want
to do this in a way that

00:15:51.660 --> 00:15:53.370
is fairly structured.

00:15:53.370 --> 00:15:56.290
So you ideally would
use complete sentences.

00:15:56.290 --> 00:15:59.340
So the reason for doing
that is that thoughts,

00:15:59.340 --> 00:16:01.530
as I mentioned earlier,
can often be fragmentary.

00:16:01.530 --> 00:16:04.110
So they pop up in
our mind almost

00:16:04.110 --> 00:16:05.740
we seemingly spontaneously.

00:16:05.740 --> 00:16:08.310
They're inhibiting
our ability to focus,

00:16:08.310 --> 00:16:10.410
or be present at work, or
family, or other things,

00:16:10.410 --> 00:16:11.790
or sleep.

00:16:11.790 --> 00:16:14.730
Writing things down
in a lot of detail

00:16:14.730 --> 00:16:18.510
does seem to have this
quality of both reducing

00:16:18.510 --> 00:16:23.190
the emotional load of whatever
it is that thought is about,

00:16:23.190 --> 00:16:26.550
as well as diminishing the
frequency of those intrusive

00:16:26.550 --> 00:16:28.000
thoughts over time.

00:16:28.000 --> 00:16:30.720
So this is far and away
different than the strategy

00:16:30.720 --> 00:16:32.970
I mentioned for the other
types of intrusive thoughts.

00:16:32.970 --> 00:16:34.980
And really it's far
and away different

00:16:34.980 --> 00:16:37.320
from the 30-day
abstinence approach

00:16:37.320 --> 00:16:39.840
that Dr. Anna Lembke was
talking about for substance

00:16:39.840 --> 00:16:41.250
or behavioral addictions.

00:16:41.250 --> 00:16:44.730
Now, of course, this process
of abstaining from thoughts

00:16:44.730 --> 00:16:48.780
or removing the addictive
nature of certain thoughts

00:16:48.780 --> 00:16:50.680
can definitely take some time.

00:16:50.680 --> 00:16:53.508
So a good example there
would be superstitions.

00:16:53.508 --> 00:16:55.050
I'll come clean
here, and I've talked

00:16:55.050 --> 00:16:57.060
about this before
on a few podcasts

00:16:57.060 --> 00:16:58.830
that when I was in
college, I developed

00:16:58.830 --> 00:17:01.830
a knock on wood superstition.

00:17:01.830 --> 00:17:04.470
Any time I'd say something that
I didn't want to happen or did

00:17:04.470 --> 00:17:07.053
want to happen, I'd say, knock
on wood, and I'd knock on wood.

00:17:07.053 --> 00:17:09.868
And then I started suppressing
the behavior mostly

00:17:09.868 --> 00:17:11.410
because it was a
little embarrassing.

00:17:11.410 --> 00:17:13.587
And then I started just
telling myself in my head,

00:17:13.587 --> 00:17:15.420
knock on wood, knock
on wood, knock on wood.

00:17:15.420 --> 00:17:18.569
And it was clearly a little
bit of an OCD type thing,

00:17:18.569 --> 00:17:21.990
but again, OCD in
air quotes here.

00:17:21.990 --> 00:17:24.750
I think it qualified
as OCD in the sense

00:17:24.750 --> 00:17:27.510
that the more I did it,
the more I wanted to do it.

00:17:27.510 --> 00:17:30.840
So I needed to go cold
turkey on the thinking,

00:17:30.840 --> 00:17:32.700
but how can you go cold
turkey on a thought?

00:17:32.700 --> 00:17:33.480
You couldn't.

00:17:33.480 --> 00:17:36.300
What I was told to do and
what worked very well for me

00:17:36.300 --> 00:17:41.100
was to just write down
the worst possible outcome

00:17:41.100 --> 00:17:42.340
that I was concerned about.

00:17:42.340 --> 00:17:46.170
So to really get close to the
nature or the underlying basis

00:17:46.170 --> 00:17:47.280
of that intrusive thought.

00:17:47.280 --> 00:17:49.320
And I raise this
because a lot of times

00:17:49.320 --> 00:17:51.708
the intrusive thought
is not, OK, I'm

00:17:51.708 --> 00:17:53.250
thinking about a
car accident, or I'm

00:17:53.250 --> 00:17:55.860
thinking about a breakup, or
I'm thinking about an exam

00:17:55.860 --> 00:17:56.880
that I have.

00:17:56.880 --> 00:17:59.190
That can be intrusive,
but a lot of times

00:17:59.190 --> 00:18:03.780
it's some kind of
nebulous, abstract,

00:18:03.780 --> 00:18:07.680
set of words, or ideas,
or images around something

00:18:07.680 --> 00:18:10.650
that happened that we saw
or heard or experienced.

00:18:10.650 --> 00:18:13.410
And by putting a lot
of clear structure

00:18:13.410 --> 00:18:17.310
to what the thought is exactly
and to putting some thought

00:18:17.310 --> 00:18:23.460
and structure onto paper
about what that pattern of not

00:18:23.460 --> 00:18:26.970
healthy thinking
relates to, people often

00:18:26.970 --> 00:18:29.580
achieve tremendous relief in
a fairly short amount of time.

00:18:29.580 --> 00:18:31.410
Sometimes just in one
session of writing it down,

00:18:31.410 --> 00:18:33.577
sometimes they need to write
it down multiple times.

00:18:33.577 --> 00:18:36.690
What you're essentially trying
to do with a intrusive thought

00:18:36.690 --> 00:18:38.280
or a trauma of
any kind is you're

00:18:38.280 --> 00:18:42.240
trying to turn a disturbing
story, that is a story that

00:18:42.240 --> 00:18:45.750
evokes a lot of
emotion and captures,

00:18:45.750 --> 00:18:49.620
it hijacks your nervous system,
into what is essentially

00:18:49.620 --> 00:18:54.300
a known but repetitive
and old, boring story

00:18:54.300 --> 00:18:56.730
where the emotional
load has been depleted.

00:18:56.730 --> 00:18:59.040
And there are, of course, I
have to highlight the fact

00:18:59.040 --> 00:19:02.640
that getting sufficient rapid
eye movement sleep we also know

00:19:02.640 --> 00:19:06.180
is very important for
removing the emotional load

00:19:06.180 --> 00:19:08.235
of traumatic experiences
and intrusive thoughts.

00:19:08.235 --> 00:19:10.860
So you really want to strive to
get the best possible sleep you

00:19:10.860 --> 00:19:11.360
can.

00:19:11.360 --> 00:19:14.040
That includes sufficient
rapid eye movement sleep.

00:19:14.040 --> 00:19:16.590
And we have multiple
zero-cost resources

00:19:16.590 --> 00:19:17.940
for that at hubermanlab.com.

00:19:17.940 --> 00:19:19.995
We have the episode
on mastery or sleep.

00:19:19.995 --> 00:19:21.870
We have the episode on
perfecting your sleep.

00:19:21.870 --> 00:19:23.340
We have the toolkit for sleep.

00:19:23.340 --> 00:19:26.070
All of which are time
stamped and all of which

00:19:26.070 --> 00:19:28.320
can be accessed to completely
zero cost to try and get

00:19:28.320 --> 00:19:31.290
your sleep as good as
possible, including lots

00:19:31.290 --> 00:19:32.760
of rapid eye movement sleep.

00:19:32.760 --> 00:19:37.290
So in order to remove intrusive
and addictive thoughts,

00:19:37.290 --> 00:19:40.350
ask yourself, is this
OCD of the classic sense?

00:19:40.350 --> 00:19:42.267
If it is, you should
see a psychiatrist.

00:19:42.267 --> 00:19:44.100
They won't necessarily
prescribe medication,

00:19:44.100 --> 00:19:47.820
but there are tools
for true OCD that are

00:19:47.820 --> 00:19:49.950
very effective in many cases.

00:19:49.950 --> 00:19:52.020
And we did the episode
on OCD which I invite

00:19:52.020 --> 00:19:55.690
you to listen to as well.

00:19:55.690 --> 00:19:57.610
You want to ask yourself,
are the thoughts

00:19:57.610 --> 00:20:00.550
disturbing or merely
intrusive and repetitive?

00:20:00.550 --> 00:20:03.310
If they're merely intrusive
and repetitive, well,

00:20:03.310 --> 00:20:05.680
then learning to focus your
attention on other things

00:20:05.680 --> 00:20:08.050
and getting better at
focusing on single things

00:20:08.050 --> 00:20:10.300
through an exercise
like mindfulness,

00:20:10.300 --> 00:20:11.440
meditation can really help.

00:20:11.440 --> 00:20:15.730
And indeed perhaps the best
use of mindfulness meditation

00:20:15.730 --> 00:20:18.520
is to improve your
level of focus.

00:20:18.520 --> 00:20:20.120
It does have other
benefits as well,

00:20:20.120 --> 00:20:22.210
but that's going to be the major
one that one will experience

00:20:22.210 --> 00:20:24.700
even with these very short 5
or 10-minute day meditations.

00:20:24.700 --> 00:20:27.100
Great data on that from
the scientific literature.

00:20:27.100 --> 00:20:31.720
And then if those intrusive
thoughts are not only intrusive

00:20:31.720 --> 00:20:33.530
but they're also
disturbing, in that case,

00:20:33.530 --> 00:20:37.390
you really want to put as
much structure and thought,

00:20:37.390 --> 00:20:40.390
believe it or not, into
what those thoughts are

00:20:40.390 --> 00:20:41.170
really about.

00:20:41.170 --> 00:20:43.900
Write them out on paper
in complete sentences

00:20:43.900 --> 00:20:48.700
and maybe do that multiple
times until the underlying

00:20:48.700 --> 00:20:50.320
emotions related
to those thoughts

00:20:50.320 --> 00:20:51.580
really start to diminish.

00:20:51.580 --> 00:20:53.380
And by doing that,
you're essentially

00:20:53.380 --> 00:20:56.170
doing your own form
of trauma therapy,

00:20:56.170 --> 00:20:57.800
for lack of a better
way to put it.

00:20:57.800 --> 00:20:59.890
And again, the data
really point to the fact

00:20:59.890 --> 00:21:03.363
that getting close to
the specific details

00:21:03.363 --> 00:21:04.780
around those
intrusive thoughts is

00:21:04.780 --> 00:21:07.450
going to be the best
way to extinguish them.

